1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,026
Why does a promise of love come

2
00:00:03,026 --> 00:00:04,331
with a small,

3
00:00:04,331 --> 00:00:05,600
clear stone?

4
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:08,025
And who decided that it should?

5
00:00:08,283 --> 00:00:10,746
The answer starts with one company,

6
00:00:10,746 --> 00:00:11,693
De Beers.

7
00:00:11,693 --> 00:00:13,508
For most of the last century,

8
00:00:13,508 --> 00:00:15,751
it controlled seventy-five to eighty

9
00:00:15,751 --> 00:00:17,983
percent of the world's rough diamonds.

10
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:20,222
And in nineteen forty-seven,

11
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:22,743
its advertising agency came up with four

12
00:00:22,743 --> 00:00:25,233
words that changed weddings forever.

13
00:00:25,463 --> 00:00:27,663
A diamond is forever.

14
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:28,458
Today,

15
00:00:28,458 --> 00:00:30,829
the company that made diamonds priceless

16
00:00:30,829 --> 00:00:31,885
is being sold off,

17
00:00:31,885 --> 00:00:33,610
at a price that would have seemed

18
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:35,934
impossible not long ago.

19
00:00:46,583 --> 00:00:49,165
How do you keep something valuable when

20
00:00:49,165 --> 00:00:51,173
the ground is full of it?

21
00:00:55,583 --> 00:00:57,848
In the early eighteen seventies,

22
00:00:57,848 --> 00:00:59,907
huge diamond mines were found around

23
00:00:59,907 --> 00:01:00,799
Kimberley,

24
00:01:00,799 --> 00:01:02,543
in South Africa.

25
00:01:02,743 --> 00:01:05,686
Suddenly there were so many diamonds that

26
00:01:05,686 --> 00:01:07,623
prices swung wildly.

27
00:01:07,813 --> 00:01:08,726
Two rivals,

28
00:01:08,726 --> 00:01:11,279
Cecil Rhodes and Barney Barnato,

29
00:01:11,279 --> 00:01:13,753
fought for control of the mines.

30
00:01:13,973 --> 00:01:15,443
In eighteen eighty-eight,

31
00:01:15,443 --> 00:01:16,414
Rhodes won,

32
00:01:16,414 --> 00:01:20,003
and De Beers Consolidated Mines was born.

33
00:01:20,253 --> 00:01:22,743
Both men agreed on one thing.

34
00:01:22,743 --> 00:01:25,168
Controlling how many diamonds came out of

35
00:01:25,168 --> 00:01:26,932
the ground was the only way to

36
00:01:26,932 --> 00:01:28,634
keep prices stable.

37
00:01:28,883 --> 00:01:31,133
So De Beers signed a deal with

38
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:33,945
ten diamond dealers in London to buy

39
00:01:33,945 --> 00:01:36,233
all of its production.

40
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:37,078
Later,

41
00:01:37,078 --> 00:01:38,965
under Ernest Oppenheimer,

42
00:01:38,965 --> 00:01:40,873
all rough sales went through a single

43
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:42,533
selling company.

44
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:45,508
It sorted the stones into thousands of

45
00:01:45,508 --> 00:01:48,977
categories and sold them at regular sales,

46
00:01:48,977 --> 00:01:49,950
called sights,

47
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:52,671
to a small circle of approved buyers,

48
00:01:52,671 --> 00:01:54,519
at prices it set.

49
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:57,591
The syndicate nearly collapsed twice,

50
00:01:57,591 --> 00:02:00,425
in the financial crash of nineteen oh

51
00:02:00,425 --> 00:02:03,343
seven and during the First World War.

52
00:02:03,503 --> 00:02:04,928
The man who rebuilt it,

53
00:02:04,928 --> 00:02:06,354
Ernest Oppenheimer,

54
00:02:06,354 --> 00:02:08,813
did it through another company.

55
00:02:09,053 --> 00:02:10,595
With American backers,

56
00:02:10,595 --> 00:02:12,742
he created a mining firm called Anglo

57
00:02:12,742 --> 00:02:14,123
American.

58
00:02:14,243 --> 00:02:15,935
By nineteen twenty-nine,

59
00:02:15,935 --> 00:02:17,863
he had used it to take over

60
00:02:17,863 --> 00:02:20,005
both the London syndicate and De Beers

61
00:02:20,005 --> 00:02:21,033
itself.

62
00:02:21,233 --> 00:02:22,803
Remember that name.

63
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:24,907
Almost a century later,

64
00:02:24,907 --> 00:02:27,470
Anglo American would be the one trying

65
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:29,002
to sell De Beers.

66
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:32,250
Let me draw how the cartel worked.

67
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:33,730
Here are the mines,

68
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:35,728
De Beers' own and others around the

69
00:02:35,728 --> 00:02:36,550
world.

70
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,197
All their stones flow into one box,

71
00:02:39,197 --> 00:02:41,250
the selling company.

72
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:41,939
From there,

73
00:02:41,939 --> 00:02:43,786
they go out to a small circle

74
00:02:43,786 --> 00:02:45,530
of approved buyers.

75
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:47,250
And here is the trick.

76
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:48,713
When demand was weak,

77
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:50,860
the box simply kept the diamonds,

78
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:53,390
or told the mines to dig less.

79
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,775
So prices went up when times were

80
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:56,264
good,

81
00:02:56,264 --> 00:02:58,151
and never came down.

82
00:02:58,417 --> 00:03:00,552
By the nineteen eighties,

83
00:03:00,552 --> 00:03:03,794
De Beers' own mines produced only about

84
00:03:03,794 --> 00:03:06,226
forty-five percent of the world's rough

85
00:03:06,226 --> 00:03:07,907
diamonds by value.

86
00:03:08,387 --> 00:03:09,852
But through its contracts,

87
00:03:09,852 --> 00:03:12,310
including with the Soviet Union,

88
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:15,290
it controlled the sale of around eighty

89
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:16,387
percent.

90
00:03:16,737 --> 00:03:18,549
It did not need to own the

91
00:03:18,549 --> 00:03:19,627
diamonds.

92
00:03:19,627 --> 00:03:21,823
It only needed to be the one

93
00:03:21,823 --> 00:03:23,399
place they were sold.

94
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:26,920
Controlling supply was half the job.

95
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,509
The other half was creating demand.

96
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:38,305
How do you make people want a

97
00:03:38,305 --> 00:03:39,174
stone?

98
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:47,589
In September nineteen thirty-eight,

99
00:03:47,589 --> 00:03:49,535
with war coming in Europe,

100
00:03:49,535 --> 00:03:51,860
diamond prices were falling.

101
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,491
Harry Oppenheimer,

102
00:03:53,491 --> 00:03:55,243
the son of De Beers' chairman,

103
00:03:55,243 --> 00:03:57,683
decided the company's future lay in

104
00:03:57,683 --> 00:03:59,170
America.

105
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,490
He traveled to Philadelphia,

106
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:04,496
to an advertising agency called N W

107
00:04:04,496 --> 00:04:05,790
Ayer and Son.

108
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,599
For almost a decade,

109
00:04:07,599 --> 00:04:10,240
the agency draped diamonds on Hollywood

110
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:11,060
stars.

111
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:13,381
But it still had no line that

112
00:04:13,381 --> 00:04:14,311
stuck.

113
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:14,994
Then,

114
00:04:14,994 --> 00:04:17,073
in nineteen forty-seven,

115
00:04:17,073 --> 00:04:20,320
a young copywriter named Frances Gerety,

116
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:21,932
according to Adweek,

117
00:04:21,932 --> 00:04:24,035
wrote four words under a photo of

118
00:04:24,035 --> 00:04:26,270
a honeymooning couple.

119
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:28,730
A diamond is forever.

120
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:31,420
De Beers adopted the line the next

121
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:31,916
year,

122
00:04:31,916 --> 00:04:34,605
and has used it ever since.

123
00:04:34,850 --> 00:04:37,075
The genius was in what the line

124
00:04:37,075 --> 00:04:38,510
connected.

125
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:39,751
Not beauty,

126
00:04:39,751 --> 00:04:40,658
not wealth,

127
00:04:40,658 --> 00:04:42,510
but love that lasts.

128
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:44,930
A diamond stopped being a gem and

129
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:46,830
became a promise.

130
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:49,017
And since grooms make up about nine

131
00:04:49,017 --> 00:04:50,773
out of ten diamond buyers,

132
00:04:50,773 --> 00:04:52,529
the ads spoke straight to them,

133
00:04:52,529 --> 00:04:55,075
with lines about how the stone reflects

134
00:04:55,075 --> 00:04:57,203
the greatness of your love.

135
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:00,076
The ads were not shy about money

136
00:05:00,076 --> 00:05:01,260
either.

137
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:04,203
A De Beers advertisement from nineteen

138
00:05:04,203 --> 00:05:07,132
fifty-seven listed a one carat diamond at

139
00:05:07,132 --> 00:05:09,159
six hundred to twelve hundred and ten

140
00:05:09,159 --> 00:05:10,210
dollars.

141
00:05:10,450 --> 00:05:11,484
At the time,

142
00:05:11,484 --> 00:05:14,932
the average American man took home about

143
00:05:14,932 --> 00:05:17,585
four hundred and seventeen dollars a

144
00:05:17,585 --> 00:05:18,450
month.

145
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:19,480
The message was clear.

146
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,550
Real love costs months of pay.

147
00:05:21,783 --> 00:05:23,856
Let me draw the two halves of

148
00:05:23,856 --> 00:05:25,233
the machine.

149
00:05:25,333 --> 00:05:26,512
On one side,

150
00:05:26,512 --> 00:05:27,691
the cartel,

151
00:05:27,691 --> 00:05:29,803
holding supply back.

152
00:05:29,823 --> 00:05:31,154
On the other side,

153
00:05:31,154 --> 00:05:32,035
the slogan,

154
00:05:32,035 --> 00:05:33,763
pushing demand up.

155
00:05:33,873 --> 00:05:34,878
Supply down,

156
00:05:34,878 --> 00:05:35,883
demand up,

157
00:05:35,883 --> 00:05:38,803
and the price in the middle rises.

158
00:05:38,833 --> 00:05:41,391
And because the stone now meant forever,

159
00:05:41,391 --> 00:05:44,103
nobody wanted to sell theirs back.

160
00:05:44,103 --> 00:05:46,222
A diamond sold once was gone from

161
00:05:46,222 --> 00:05:48,146
the market for good.

162
00:05:48,383 --> 00:05:49,567
For decades,

163
00:05:49,567 --> 00:05:52,583
the machine worked almost perfectly.

164
00:05:52,783 --> 00:05:55,944
Then the world changed around it.

165
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:04,340
Can a cartel survive the end of

166
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:06,778
the world it was built for?

167
00:06:10,583 --> 00:06:13,723
There was always one awkward fact.

168
00:06:13,723 --> 00:06:14,959
America,

169
00:06:14,959 --> 00:06:17,230
the market De Beers worked so hard

170
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:17,962
to win,

171
00:06:17,962 --> 00:06:19,823
had antitrust laws.

172
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:21,853
In nineteen ninety-four,

173
00:06:21,853 --> 00:06:24,511
the US government indicted the company for

174
00:06:24,511 --> 00:06:26,964
conspiring to fix the prices of industrial

175
00:06:26,964 --> 00:06:27,973
diamonds.

176
00:06:28,153 --> 00:06:28,893
For years,

177
00:06:28,893 --> 00:06:30,397
the case went nowhere,

178
00:06:30,397 --> 00:06:32,693
because the court had no hold over

179
00:06:32,693 --> 00:06:33,803
De Beers.

180
00:06:34,143 --> 00:06:36,404
Only in two thousand four did a

181
00:06:36,404 --> 00:06:39,394
De Beers company finally plead guilty,

182
00:06:39,394 --> 00:06:41,403
and pay a fine of ten million

183
00:06:41,403 --> 00:06:42,506
dollars.

184
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:43,853
Meanwhile,

185
00:06:43,853 --> 00:06:47,000
the walls around supply were falling.

186
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:49,176
When the Soviet Union broke up in

187
00:06:49,176 --> 00:06:51,327
the early nineteen nineties,

188
00:06:51,327 --> 00:06:53,066
Moscow lost its tight grip on the

189
00:06:53,066 --> 00:06:54,970
country's diamond mines.

190
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:56,778
In nineteen ninety-eight,

191
00:06:56,778 --> 00:06:59,427
Canada opened its first diamond mine,

192
00:06:59,427 --> 00:07:01,207
and its owners sold most of the

193
00:07:01,207 --> 00:07:02,920
stones themselves.

194
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:05,260
More and more diamonds were reaching the

195
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,141
market without passing through De Beers.

196
00:07:08,383 --> 00:07:11,233
So De Beers changed strategy.

197
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:13,369
It sold off its great stockpile of

198
00:07:13,369 --> 00:07:14,252
